1
00:00:01,030 --> 00:00:02,251
Hello again! I'm Holly.

2
00:00:02,251 --> 00:00:05,048
If you've been following this
program from start to finish,

3
00:00:05,048 --> 00:00:07,051
you may remember me
from an earlier course

4
00:00:07,051 --> 00:00:10,301
where I shared a bit about accessibility
and why it's so

5
00:00:10,301 --> 00:00:13,098
important to keep it in
mind as a project manager.

6
00:00:13,098 --> 00:00:16,358
So far, you've learned
the importance of managing,

7
00:00:16,358 --> 00:00:18,885
communicating, and measuring quality.

8
00:00:18,885 --> 00:00:23,404
This is a great opportunity to also
think about how to ensure the ways you

9
00:00:23,404 --> 00:00:25,149
are collecting feedback

10
00:00:25,149 --> 00:00:29,440
and the processes in place to measure
quality are fair and accessible.

11
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:32,817
First, if you are collecting
feedback through live interviews,

12
00:00:32,817 --> 00:00:37,584
be sure to include an offer to provide
accommodations in your correspondence when

13
00:00:37,584 --> 00:00:39,114
setting up the sessions.

14
00:00:39,114 --> 00:00:42,539
You may get requests for
live captioning or an interpreter.

15
00:00:42,539 --> 00:00:45,106
Other folks, such as those with anxiety or

16
00:00:45,106 --> 00:00:49,043
on the autism spectrum,
may ask to see the questions in advance

17
00:00:49,043 --> 00:00:52,511
so that they have time to think about and
prepare their answers.

18
00:00:52,511 --> 00:00:56,906
Remember, what works for one person
may not work for another person,

19
00:00:56,906 --> 00:01:00,125
even if those two people
have the same disability.

20
00:01:00,125 --> 00:01:03,183
If you're conducting
an interview on location,

21
00:01:03,183 --> 00:01:06,106
examine the space with
an accessibility lens,

22
00:01:06,106 --> 00:01:09,941
like ensuring there's an accessible
path into the building and to the room

23
00:01:09,941 --> 00:01:14,020
and that hallways are clear of
clutter that could block someone using

24
00:01:14,020 --> 00:01:14,940
a wheelchair

25
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:18,407
or a walker or someone with
a visual impairment from easily and

26
00:01:18,407 --> 00:01:19,817
safely moving through.

27
00:01:19,817 --> 00:01:23,612
If sending a survey or
collecting feedback using technology,

28
00:01:23,612 --> 00:01:27,064
check that the systems you
are using are fully accessible.

29
00:01:27,064 --> 00:01:32,218
If you're unsure, contact the system
owner and ask if they are compliant

30
00:01:32,218 --> 00:01:36,724
with the latest Web Content
Accessibility Guidelines, or WCAG.

31
00:01:36,724 --> 00:01:38,770
Be prepared to provide questions and

32
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:41,748
collect responses in
an alternate manner, if needed.

33
00:01:41,748 --> 00:01:45,495
Beyond collecting feedback,
as a project manager, it's important for

34
00:01:45,495 --> 00:01:49,314
you to make accessibility part of
the conversation from the beginning,

35
00:01:49,314 --> 00:01:53,400
especially if your project pertains
to a process or a product.

36
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:58,203
Oftentimes, incorporating accessible
features into a product is overlooked or

37
00:01:58,203 --> 00:02:00,504
left to the final stages of a project.

38
00:02:00,504 --> 00:02:04,548
and can lead to serious implications,
like launch delays, or worse,

39
00:02:04,548 --> 00:02:08,527
a product that can't be used by
a percentage of the population.

40
00:02:08,527 --> 00:02:13,533
Ensure your developers are familiar with
accessibility requirements at the start.

41
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:17,484
If they're not, help connect them with
appropriate resources or experts.

42
00:02:17,484 --> 00:02:22,080
Include testers with various disabilities
in your usability testing whenever

43
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:22,774
possible,

44
00:02:22,774 --> 00:02:25,451
and at the very least,
have the product tested for

45
00:02:25,451 --> 00:02:27,762
adherence to accessibility guidelines.

46
00:02:27,762 --> 00:02:32,003
In summary, think about
accessibility early and often, and

47
00:02:32,003 --> 00:02:35,683
encourage others on your
project team to do so, too.

48
00:02:35,683 --> 00:02:39,248
Coming up, you will practice
ways to measure quality and

49
00:02:39,248 --> 00:02:44,460
learn more about managing changes, risk,
and so much more. Keep up the great work.
